**Blocking — defeats the optimisation this PR is selling.**

`context.select` decides whether to rebuild by comparing the previous and new selector output with `==`. In Dart, `List` uses **identity** equality (`identical`), not structural equality, so this selector returns a brand-new `List` instance on every `BackgroundPublishBloc` emit — including every progress tick. The result: the grid still rebuilds on every tick, exactly the behaviour the PR description says it eliminates. The de-duplication loop, `Image.file`, etc. all keep running per tick.

The records inside (`_ActiveUpload`) do compare by value (Dart 3 record `==`), so the fix is to wrap the result in a value-equal collection. Two options:

1. Wrap with a small class that overrides `==` via `listEquals` (Equatable-style):

   ```dart
   class _ActiveUploadList {
     const _ActiveUploadList(this.uploads);
     final List<_ActiveUpload> uploads;
     @override
     bool operator ==(Object other) =>
         other is _ActiveUploadList && listEquals(uploads, other.uploads);
     @override
     int get hashCode => Object.hashAll(uploads);
   }
   ```

   Then `context.select<..., _ActiveUploadList>(...).uploads`.

2. Or use `package:collection`'s `IList` / `EqualityNotifier` patterns already in use elsewhere.

Either way, please add a `blocTest`/widget test that pumps two emits with the same upload shape but different progress and asserts the grid widget rebuild count stays at 1 — otherwise this regression will silently come back.

